<main id="main">
<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-chart.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-chart-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-chart.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Insurance terms</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Terms, Defined in Plain English</h1><p class="lede">A searchable glossary of 50 words you’ll meet in Minnesota policies, estimates and appraisal awards.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" id="gloss-count" aria-live="polite">50 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-B">B</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-G">G</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-J">J</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-Q">Q</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-T">T</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="term" id="g-A"><dt>Actual Cash Value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay this amount first, before repairs are finished.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Additional Living Expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while a home is unlivable after a covered loss; often called loss of use or Coverage D.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Amount of Loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of a covered loss. It is the question an appraisal panel is set up to decide.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal</dt><dd>A policy-based process in which two appraisers and, if needed, an umpire determine the amount of a covered loss when the insured and insurer disagree.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal Award</dt><dd>The written decision, signed by any two of the three panel members, that sets the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-B"><dt>Bad Faith</dt><dd>A general term for unreasonable claim handling by an insurer. In Minnesota, claims of this kind are legal matters for an attorney, not for an appraisal panel.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-C"><dt>Causation</dt><dd>What caused the damage, such as hail versus age-related wear. Minnesota courts have allowed appraisers to address causation as part of the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Claim Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or increase pricing on an existing estimate, usually supported by new photos, bids or code requirements.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage A (Dwelling)</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that pays to repair or rebuild the house itself and attached structures.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage B (Other Structures)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures on the property, such as a garage, shed or fence.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-D"><dt>Declarations Page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the named insured, property address, coverage limits, deductibles and policy period.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss the policyholder pays before insurance pays. Some policies use a separate wind or hail deductible.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Department of Commerce</dt><dd>The Minnesota Department of Commerce, the state agency that regulates insurers and licenses agents, adjusters and public adjusters.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to arrive at actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm with straight-line winds, such as the one that crossed southern Minnesota on May 12, 2022.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Disinterested Appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial stake in the outcome of the claim beyond a reasonable fee for services.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Drip Edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing installed along roof edges to direct water into the gutters and protect the fascia and deck edge.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-E"><dt>Estimate Review</dt><dd>An independent check of an insurer’s or contractor’s estimate for missing items, measurement errors and pricing problems.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-F"><dt>FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The Minnesota FAIR Plan, which offers basic property insurance when coverage isn’t available in the voluntary market.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Fascia</dt><dd>The vertical board along the roof edge where gutters attach; often damaged by hail, wind and ice.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-G"><dt>Gutter Apron</dt><dd>A flashing piece that tucks under shingles and laps into the gutter to keep water from running behind it.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-H"><dt>Hail Bruise</dt><dd>A soft spot in a shingle where hail has fractured the mat beneath the granules, even if no hole is visible.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-I"><dt>Ice and Water Shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof membrane installed at eaves, valleys and penetrations to resist leaks from ice dams and wind-driven rain.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Ice Dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps meltwater, which can back up under shingles and leak into walls and ceilings.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Insured</dt><dd>The person or entity covered under the policy. See also named insured.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-J"><dt>Joint Inspection</dt><dd>A site visit attended by both appraisers, and sometimes the umpire, to measure and document the damage together.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-L"><dt>Line Item</dt><dd>A single entry in an estimate, such as removal of one square of shingles, with quantity, unit price and total.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Loss of Use</dt><dd>Coverage for added living expenses or lost rental income when property can’t be used after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs reasonably match in color, texture and appearance.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mortgagee Clause</dt><dd>Policy wording that protects the lender’s interest; it is why claim checks often name the mortgage company as a payee.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-N"><dt>Named Insured</dt><dd>The person or entity listed on the declarations page as the policyholder.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-O"><dt>Ordinance or Law Coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to bring repaired portions of a building up to current building codes.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Overhead and Profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for managing a project and running a business, often 10 percent each, when the job requires coordinating several trades.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-P"><dt>Proof of Loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, which some policies require within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Public Adjuster</dt><dd>A professional licensed by the Minnesota Department of Commerce to represent policyholders in preparing and negotiating a claim.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-Q"><dt>Quade v. Secura</dt><dd>A 2012 Minnesota Supreme Court decision recognizing that appraisers may decide causation as part of the amount of loss, while coverage remains for courts.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-R"><dt>Recoverable Depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation withheld from an initial payment that can be recovered once repairs are completed, if the policy provides replacement cost coverage.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Replacement Cost Value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without a deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-S"><dt>Scope of Loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed, before pricing is applied.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Sewer Backup Endorsement</dt><dd>Optional coverage for damage caused by water backing up through drains or sewers; many base policies exclude it.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Square (Roofing)</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Steel Siding</dt><dd>Painted steel lap siding common on older Minnesota homes; dents easily in hail and is often hard to match once discontinued.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Suit Limitation</dt><dd>A policy condition limiting how long you have to sue the insurer, commonly two years after the loss in Minnesota forms; confirm with an attorney.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Supplemental Payment</dt><dd>An additional payment the insurer issues after approving a supplement or after an appraisal award.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-T"><dt>Tear-Off</dt><dd>Removal of existing roofing layers down to the deck before new roofing is installed.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Underlayment</dt><dd>The protective layer between the roof deck and shingles, such as felt or synthetic sheet.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-V"><dt>Valley</dt><dd>The internal angle where two roof slopes meet; a common point for ice and water leaks.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-W"><dt>Wind Deductible</dt><dd>A separate deductible for wind or hail losses, sometimes stated as a percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="term" id="g-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers, adjusters and contractors to build line-item repair estimates with regional pricing.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sticky"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-t">Talk it through</h2><p>A free first conversation about your claim. No pressure, no obligation.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> +1 (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-sky btn-block" href="/contact-us/">Send claim details</a><small>Mon–Fri 8am–6pm Central</small></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-t">Resources</h2><ul class="side-links"><li><a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">Appraisal process guide</a></li><li><a href="/common-questions/">Common questions</a></li><li><a href="/insurance-terms/" aria-current="page">Insurance terms</a></li><li><a href="/learning-center/">Learning center</a></li></ul></div></div></aside></div>
